            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Grass primarily symbolizes the brevity, frailty, and mortality of human life, with some recognition that it represents humanity or flesh collectively.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Grass symbolizes the individual mortal (a man or flesh) as well as the field that God farms unto harvest.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The transience and frailty of human life, or humanity itself.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The individual mortal (flesh) and the field God farms.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus restricts the symbol primarily to the attribute of frailty or transience of humanity, while the book identifies it substantively as the mortal individual ('flesh') and broadens the symbol to include the 'field' God farms for harvest.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"No direct contradiction; the book accepts the identification with 'flesh' (from Isaiah 40) but removes the limitation to the attribute of transience, expanding it to agricultural harvest imagery.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book preserves the consensus connection to humanity/flesh based on Isaiah 40, but shifts the focus from the attribute of mortality to the substantive entity (the individual mortal) and expands the extension to include the agricultural harvest of the world, relying on Matthew 13.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Matthew 13:38 defines the 'field' as the world and the 'good seed' as the children of the kingdom, but does not explicitly mention 'grass' (Greek *chortos*), making the extension of the symbol of grass to mean the 'field God farms' textually indirect based on this verse alone.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
